Debian packaging for Prime Agent
===============================

This downstream package definition is extracted from
packaging/homebrew/Formula/prime-agent.rb. It uses the checkout's upstream
version (currently 0.10.1), rather than the formula's future 1.0.0 release.
Update debian/changelog with each upstream release.

Build inputs
------------

Build natively in a Debian or Ubuntu environment with debhelper 13, a C
compiler/linker (build-essential), Python 3, and packaged Cargo and rustc
1.88 or newer. The locked dependency set requires that Rust version.
dpkg-checkbuilddeps checks installed Debian packages; a rustup toolchain
alone does not satisfy the cargo/rustc Build-Depends declarations.
Ubuntu versioned cargo-1.89 and rustc-1.89 packages are also supported.
Select them with CARGO=cargo-1.89 RUSTC=rustc-1.89 when invoking the build.

Like the formula, this definition uses cargo build --release --locked.
Cargo may download locked dependencies. This is downstream packaging, not
an offline Debian archive submission with individually packaged Rust crates.
The runtime depends on a Debian package providing uv; make that package
available for the target distribution before installing Prime Agent.

Release source archives already contain models.bundled.json and
mcp-services.bundled.json at their root. From an ordinary working checkout,
prepare a catalog snapshot explicitly, using the existing release tool:

    python3 scripts/release/bundle_catalog.py generate --network \
        --out target/catalog-assets

A local catalog checkout can be used instead of the network by replacing
--network with --catalog-dir /path/to/prime-agent-catalog. Synthetic
--fixture snapshots are for packaging tests only. The build validates both
assets and fails if either is missing or invalid; it does not fetch catalogs.

Build architecture-dependent binaries without signing:

    dpkg-checkbuilddeps -B
    make package-deb

The Makefile target passes CATALOG_ASSETS_DIR (default target/catalog-assets)
to dpkg-buildpackage -B --no-sign. To use a different prepared snapshot:

    make package-deb CATALOG_ASSETS_DIR=/path/to/catalog-assets

For a release source archive, run make package-deb CATALOG_ASSETS_DIR=.
Run the build as an unprivileged user. The package build runs a --version
smoke test unless DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS contains nocheck. Run make check
separately for the repository's full compilation, lint, and test gates.

Only debian/cargo-target, debian/catalog-assets, and debhelper's generated
files are removed by the package clean step. Developer target/ artifacts,
catalog inputs, and local source changes are preserved.

Payload
-------

/usr/bin/prime-agent links to /usr/lib/prime-agent/prime-agent. The same
directory holds prime-agent-runtime (pyproject.toml and src/), skills/,
both bundled catalogs, LICENSE, and README.md. Keeping the sidecars beside
the real executable preserves the formula's resource lookup behavior.
Runtime tests and its development lockfile are omitted, matching the
release payload policy; Python caches and virtual environments are excluded.
Debhelper derives ELF library dependencies and handles debug symbols.

Binary builds need no upstream orig tarball. Source builds use 3.0 (quilt)
and require a matching prime-agent_<version>.orig.tar.gz in the parent
directory, with downstream changes represented as Debian patches.
